  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="3.3">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Swer">Swer</ref> 3.3</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="3.3" corresp="#wit-b">7 swa ic orf hæbbe, swa hit me se sealde, ðe hit to syllanne ahte.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="3.3" corresp="#wit-q">Et sic pecus habeo, sicut mihi uendidit, cui iure pertinebat.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="3.3" corresp="#wit-h">7 swa ic orf hæbbe, swa hit me se sealde, ðe hit to syllanne agte.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#unattributed" corresp="#wit-h">[Alternatively:] And so I [do] have cattle that were sold to me by [someone] who [legally] owned them.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">Beziehungsweise: só besitz ich Vieh, wie dér es mir veräussert hat, der es zu veräussern befugt war.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">Or: I possess the cattle just as he sold it to me who was entitled to sell it.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#liebermann" n="397"><ref target="clause:Becwaeth_2">Becwæð 2</ref></note>
        <note type="comparisonPrinted" source="#liebermann">vgl. Becwæð 2</note>
        <note type="comparedBy" source="#wormald-mel" n="385"><ref target="clause:Becwaeth_2">Becwæð 2</ref></note>
        <note type="editorial">B lacks ‘se’; Liebermann supplies it. H reads ‘agte’ (so). Liebermann compares Becw 2.</note>
      </div>
    </body>
